<edge source="iran_us_regional_war::escalation_pressure" target="global_energy_shift::crude_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising systemic escalation risk raises market risk premia and speculative buying (and prompts hedging by consumers and traders), producing upward pressure and episodic spikes in benchmark crude prices.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_us_regional_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_us_regional_war::escalation_pressure" target="russia_ukraine_war::alliance_support_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in Iran–US escalation ties down US diplomatic bandwidth and political capital (and forces prioritization of Middle East contingency planning), causing Washington and key European partners to scale back or delay political/diplomatic backing and high-profile initiatives for Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_us_regional_war</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_us_regional_war::escalation_pressure" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_control" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising systemic escalation gives policymakers political cover and urgency to widen and tighten export controls, CFIUS‑style screening and compliance regimes for advanced compute and chips, accelerating the introduction of stricter regulatory barriers affecting US‑China tech flows.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_us_regional_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
