<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_price_volatility" target="global_energy_shift::crude_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Spikes in short- and medium-term energy price volatility increase risk premia and induce precautionary buying and inventory hoarding by market participants, exerting upward pressure on benchmark crude price levels.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_price_volatility" target="iran_us_regional_war::escalation_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Large and sustained volatility or spikes in global energy prices increase the economic and political payoff to coercive disruption (and in some cases state revenues), incentivizing Gulf actors and Iran to take riskier, pressure-inducing actions that raise systemic escalation pressure between regional actors and the U.S.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_us_regional_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::energy_price_volatility" target="market_monetary_stress::long_term_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Sharp, sustained swings in energy prices raise uncertainty about future inflation and fiscal backstops, increasing the term premium investors demand on long-duration government bonds and pushing long-term yields up.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">market_monetary_stress</data>
