<edge source="global_energy_shift::rerouting_rate" target="market_monetary_stress::fiscal_financing_pressur" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher rates of ship rerouting impose additional transit costs and delivery delays that force energy‑exporting governments to provide emergency liquidity support, subsidies or bridge financing for exporters and importers, raising near‑term borrowing needs and rollover pressure.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_shift</data>
  <data key="d40">market_monetary_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_shift::rerouting_rate" target="russia_ukraine_war::energy_price_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher rates of rerouting lengthen transit times, raise freight and insurance cost uncertainty, and create uneven delivery timing, which amplifies short- to medium-term swings in energy commodity prices.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_shift</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_shift::rerouting_rate" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::chip_production_capacity" id="0">
